    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "retenir",
     "title": "Changes of state",
     "md": "**Melting**: solid to liquid (ice to water). **Freezing**: liquid to solid (water to ice at 0 °C). **Evaporation**: liquid to gas (wet clothes drying). **Boiling**: water changes to steam fast at 100 °C. **Condensation**: gas to liquid (drops on a cold glass). Heat makes things melt or evaporate; cooling makes them freeze or condense."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "- Thinking that air is nothing: air is a gas and takes up space.\n- Saying that steam is the white cloud you see: the white is tiny water droplets; steam itself is invisible.\n- Saying that a powder (like flour) is a liquid because it can be poured: each grain is a solid.\n- Mixing melting (solid to liquid) with evaporation (liquid to gas)."
    },
