    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "retenir",
     "title": "Heat and sound",
     "md": "**Heat** flows from hot things to cold things. **Metals** are good **conductors** of heat (a metal spoon in hot soup gets hot). **Wood, plastic and cloth** are **insulators**: pot handles and oven gloves are made of them. **Sound** is made when things **vibrate** (a drum skin, a guitar string). It travels through air, water and solids, but **not through empty space**. Loud sounds can hurt the ears."
    },
    {
     "type": "key",
     "style": "pieges",
     "title": "Common mistakes",
     "md": "- Thinking that a shadow is made of light: it is a place where light is blocked.\n- Touching a hot pot without a holder.\n- Thinking that sound needs no material: it needs something to travel through.\n- Playing loud music or shouting right next to a person's ear."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 1",
     "statement": "Why is the handle of a metal pot made of wood or plastic?",
     "steps": [
      "Metal conducts heat, so a metal handle would burn the hand.",
      "Wood and plastic are insulators."
     ],
     "answer": "They do not conduct heat, so the handle stays cool."
    },
    {
     "type": "example",
     "title": "Example 2",
     "statement": "A child stands in the sun and has a shadow behind her. Where is the Sun?",
     "steps": [
      "A shadow forms on the side away from the light.",
      "The Sun is in front of her, opposite the shadow."
     ],
     "answer": "In front of her (opposite the shadow)."
    }
   ],
